WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.440
Last time, one pyruvate entered a swinging-arm complex and left as acetyl-CoA,

2
00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:08.180
carbon dioxide and NADH.

3
00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.800
That acetyl-CoA leaves as a wound spring.

4
00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:14.660
Yet no ATP went in.

5
00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:16.840
So who wound it?

6
00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.260
To find out, keep the energy books.

7
00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:26.220
Pyruvate starts at zero, in kilojoules per mole.

8
00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:30.340
Downhill releases energy, and uphill spends it.

9
00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:34.080
We list three entries, not in the order they happen.

10
00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:37.050
First entry: the oxidation.

11
00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:41.670
Two carbons go from aldehyde level to acid level.

12
00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:45.130
Their electrons end on NAD⁺.

13
00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:48.490
That falls about 50 kJ/mol.

14
00:00:49.583 --> 00:00:55.683
Second entry: the acetyl group is stored as a thioester on coenzyme A, not set free

15
00:00:55.683 --> 00:00:59.403
as acetate. That costs about 31, uphill.

16
00:01:00.683 --> 00:01:02.743
It spends most of the oxidation's 50.

17
00:01:03.917 --> 00:01:08.257
Third entry: carbon dioxide leaves, about 14 more downhill.

18
00:01:09.537 --> 00:01:12.237
That one is found by difference, so it is approximate.

19
00:01:13.697 --> 00:01:16.997
Add them up: minus −50, plus +31, minus −14.

20
00:01:18.377 --> 00:01:19.917
About minus −33 overall.

21
00:01:21.067 --> 00:01:22.507
So here is the answer.

22
00:01:23.827 --> 00:01:26.327
The oxidation is the main energy source.

23
00:01:27.547 --> 00:01:30.327
It pays for the NADH and for the thioester.

24
00:01:31.667 --> 00:01:36.007
What is left, together with losing carbon dioxide, makes the gate one-way.

25
00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:45.210
How one-way? A drop of about 33 gives an equilibrium constant near 4 × 10⁵ at body

26
00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:49.610
temperature. In practice, the reaction runs only forward.

27
00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:53.390
In animals, no enzyme runs it backward.

28
00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:56.160
Now look at the spring itself.

29
00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:02.520
Picture two esters: an ordinary one, joined through oxygen, and this one, joined

30
00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:05.560
through sulfur. Split each with water.

31
00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:08.180
Which releases more energy?

32
00:02:14.233 --> 00:02:18.873
The sulfur one. The oxygen ester gives about 21.

33
00:02:20.313 --> 00:02:25.153
The thioester gives about 31, roughly matching ATP's 30.5.

34
00:02:26.393 --> 00:02:29.873
That is the same 31 the oxidation paid to build it.

35
00:02:30.817 --> 00:02:34.757
Why? Not because energy hides in the bond.

36
00:02:36.057 --> 00:02:38.137
Breaking a bond always costs energy.

37
00:02:39.617 --> 00:02:43.997
Sulfur is larger than oxygen, so it overlaps the carbonyl carbon poorly.

38
00:02:45.377 --> 00:02:47.777
The thioester gets little resonance help.

39
00:02:49.057 --> 00:02:52.977
Its products, acetate and free coenzyme A, are much more stable.

40
00:02:54.297 --> 00:02:56.877
The thioester is a boulder perched on a cliff.

41
00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:00.460
So no ATP wound the spring.

42
00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:08.300
Oxidizing two carbons one notch further did, and it paid for the NADH too.

43
00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:12.740
Acetyl-CoA carries that push into the citric acid cycle's first step, and into

44
00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:13.940
fatty-acid synthesis.

45
00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:17.180
What remained keeps the traffic one-way.
